what virus puts TRANS.TBL file in every folder, even write protected cds?
Apr 24

TRANS.TBL is not malware — it's a legitimate filesystem translation table created by the mkisofs / genisoimage toolchain when building ISO 9660 images.

What it is:

    Generated during ISO creation when the -T flag is passed to mkisofs
    Maps the 8.3 DOS-truncated ISO 9660 filenames back to their original long names
    Read by the installer/OS to resolve the real filenames at runtime
    Present in every directory because each directory needs its own translation table
    Baked into the ISO at build time — that's why it appears on write-protected CDs; it was written before the disc was pressed

Why Fedora 9 specifically has it everywhere:

Red Hat's ISO build scripts used -T by default in that era. Every folder gets its own TRANS.TBL mapping the Joliet/RockRidge names to ISO 9660 compliant names.

Forensic note: On a write-protected CD, presence of TRANS.TBL is a build artifact, not an infection vector. If you're seeing TRANS.TBL appearing on a writable medium or live filesystem that wasn't built with mkisofs, that would be anomalous and worth flagging — mkisofs/genisoimage are the only legitimate producers of this file.

No AV action needed on the Fedora 9 disc.

