That makes sense — stage2.img on Fedora 9 is not a directly mountable ext2/cramfs in the traditional sense. It's a compressed squashfs or a loopback image containing an installer payload, not a full root filesystem.

Check what's actually inside each image:
bash

# What does file say about stage2
file $CD/images/stage2.img

# Try to read the first few bytes
xxd $CD/images/stage2.img | head -20

# Check for squashfs magic (0x73717368)
hexdump -C $CD/images/stage2.img | head -5

Fedora 9 stage2 is actually a loopback ext2 containing a squashfs:
bash

# Mount the outer ext2 first
mkdir -p /mnt/stage2-outer
mount -t ext2 $CD/images/stage2.img /mnt/stage2-outer -o loop

# Then look what's inside
ls /mnt/stage2-outer

# Usually contains squashfs.img or similar
file /mnt/stage2-outer/*

# Mount the inner squashfs
mkdir -p /mnt/stage2-inner
mount -t squashfs /mnt/stage2-outer/squashfs.img /mnt/stage2-inner -o loop

The actual root with /bin /lib is in the inner mount. That's what you chroot into:
bash

mount --bind /dev  /mnt/stage2-inner/dev
mount --bind /proc /mnt/stage2-inner/proc
mount --bind /sys  /mnt/stage2-inner/sys
chroot /mnt/stage2-inner /bin/bash

