Honest answer — several problems with that approach:

fdformat issue: fdformat only works on actual block devices (/dev/fd0). It will reject a flat file. It's also formatting, not writing — it just lays down sector markers.

The real floppy size problem: A standard floppy is 1,474,560 bytes — 1.44MB. kernel2.6.23 from Fedora 9 is ~2.5MB compressed. It does not fit on a floppy image. That's why the script originally had -fda and -kernel as separate flags — QEMU's -kernel direct boot bypasses the floppy entirely.

What actually works for each component:

Kernel — use QEMU direct boot, not floppy:
bash

# Already handled by your script's -kernel flag
-kernel /home/qemu/kernel2.6.23.img
# QEMU loads this directly into memory, no block device needed

Floppy — repurpose as DSL myconf persistence instead:
bash

# Format the floppy image as ext2 via loopback
mkfs.ext2 /home/qemu/floppy.img
# QEMU presents it as /dev/fd0 inside the guest
# DSL reads myconf from floppy automatically on boot

ROM boot from parallelflash:
bash

# pflash is the right approach but needs proper firmware
# Copy QEMU's seabios into it
dd if=/usr/share/qemu/bios.bin \
   of=/home/qemu/parallelflash.img \
   conv=notrunc bs=1 \
   seek=$((128*1024*1024 - $(stat -c%s /usr/share/qemu/bios.bin)))

QEMU maps pflash to the top of the 32-bit address space where x86 expects BIOS — this is correct ROM boot behavior.

initrd from hdb:
