Given confirmed compromise, do this forensically — observe before killing so you have a record.

Step 1 — Snapshot what's running before touching anything:
bash

ps auxf --sort=-%mem > /tmp/ps_snapshot.txt
top -b -n 1 >> /tmp/ps_snapshot.txt
cat /proc/meminfo >> /tmp/ps_snapshot.txt
cat /proc/loadavg >> /tmp/ps_snapshot.txt
lsof +L1 >> /tmp/ps_snapshot.txt  # deleted-but-open files (memfd/fileless)

Step 2 — Identify what's actually eating resources:
bash

# CPU + memory top offenders
ps auxf --sort=-%cpu | head -30
ps auxf --sort=-%mem | head -30

# Anything running from memfd or deleted inodes
ls -la /proc/*/exe 2>/dev/null | grep deleted
ls -la /proc/*/fd 2>/dev/null | grep memfd

Step 3 — Check for known compromise indicators before killing:
bash

# Unusual parent/child trees
pstree -p

# Processes with no associated binary on disk
for pid in /proc/[0-9]*/exe; do
    readlink $pid 2>/dev/null | grep -E '(deleted|memfd)' && echo "SUSPECT: $pid"
done

# Network activity from unexpected processes
ss -tulnp
netstat -tulnp

Step 4 — Safe targeted kills, not blanket killall:
bash

# Kill by specific PID after reviewing snapshot
# DO NOT use killall or pkill blindly on compromised system
# Adversary may have renamed processes to look like system daemons

kill -9 <PID>

# Services safe to stop on F9 given your use case
service cups stop        # printing
service bluetooth stop   # BT daemon
service avahi-daemon stop # mDNS
service gpm stop         # mouse in console
service haldaemon stop   # hardware abstraction - careful
service NetworkManager stop  # if using manual network
service xfs stop         # X font server if not using X

Step 5 — Free cached memory cleanly:
bash

sync
echo 3 > /proc/sys/vm/drop_caches

Step 6 — Verify fan response:
bash

# Check temps
cat /proc/acpi/thermal_zone/*/temperature 2>/dev/null
sensors 2>/dev/null

# Check disk I/O — if something is hammering disk that's separate from CPU
iostat -x 1 5
iotop -b -n 3

Paste the ps auxf --sort=-%cpu output — I want to see what's actually running before we decide what to kill. On a compromised F9 box, high fan = either crypto mining process, exfil operation, or your own SDAR tools running. Need to see the tree.

