Those are all running as KDE session processes under your user — knotify4, kded4, konsole are KDE4 daemons, and krfcommd/btdelconn/btaddcon are Bluetooth RFCOMM daemons. The S state means sleeping, S1 means sleeping in an interruptible wait tied to the session.

They won't die with kill because:

    KDE daemons are respawning via kdeinit4/dbus
    BT processes may be kernel threads or have a watchdog parent
    On compromised system, respawn may be intentional

Step 1 — Identify what's protecting them:
bash

# Find parent PIDs
ps auxf | grep -E 'knotify|kded|konsole|krfcommd|btdel|btadd'

# Check if kernel thread
cat /proc/<PID>/status | grep -E 'Name|Pid|PPid|State'

# Check respawn source
cat /proc/<PID>/cmdline | tr '\0' ' '

Step 2 — Kill KDE session respawner first:
bash

# Kill kdeinit4 — this is what respawns knotify4/kded4
pkill -9 kdeinit4
pkill -9 klauncher
pkill -9 kded4

# Then kill dbus session so it stops restarting them
pkill -9 dbus-daemon

Step 3 — BT processes that survive kill -9:
bash

# Check if they are kernel threads (no exe link)
ls -la /proc/<PID>/exe

# If kernel thread, must unload module
lsmod | grep -E 'bluetooth|rfcomm|bnep|l2cap'

# Unload in order
rmmod rfcomm
rmmod bnep
rmmod l2cap
rmmod bluetooth

Step 4 — If rmmod fails due to module in use:
bash

# Force unload — on compromised system acceptable
rmmod -f rfcomm
rmmod -f bnep
rmmod -f l2cap
rmmod -f bluetooth

# Verify gone
lsmod | grep bluetooth

Step 5 — If BT processes still survive after module removal:
bash

# They are not legitimate kernel threads
# Document before next step
cat /proc/<PID>/maps >> /tmp/ps_snapshot.txt
cat /proc/<PID>/smaps >> /tmp/ps_snapshot.txt
cat /proc/<PID>/net/tcp >> /tmp/ps_snapshot.txt

# Then kill the entire process group
kill -9 -<PGID>
# Get PGID from ps output, column 4

Step 6 — Prevent KDE restart without rebooting:
bash

# Remove KDE autostart entries
rm -f ~/.kde/Autostart/*
rm -f ~/.config/autostart/*

# Kill X session entirely if you don't need GUI for QEMU work
init 3
# Drops to runlevel 3, kills entire X/KDE session
# All KDE daemons die with the session

init 3 is the cleanest option here — drops X entirely, KDE session dies, BT daemons lose their parent, fan should drop immediately. QEMU runs fine from console. That's the recommended path on a compromised box anyway — no GUI attack surface.

